\chapter{BASIC Statements: Comprehensive Reference}

This chapter provides detailed documentation for all 17 core BASIC statements supported by the EightBol BASIC frontend, including complete syntax, semantics, numeric type handling, and executable examples.

\section{Overview of Numeric Types}

Throughout this chapter, examples demonstrate three numeric types:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY}: Signed or unsigned binary integers (16-bit or 32-bit)
\item \textbf{DECIMAL}: Binary-Coded Decimal (BCD), supporting 4-digit numbers per byte with signed/unsigned variants
\item \textbf{DISPLAY}: Character-encoded numeric strings (e.g., ``12345'' stored as ASCII digits)
\end{itemize}

Type conversions are performed implicitly during assignment, with BINARY being the default unless otherwise specified. All examples in this chapter use the EightBol COBOL intermediate representation syntax.

\subsection{BINARY Type Details}

The BINARY type represents integers in the processor's native binary representation. For 16-bit architectures (6502, Z80, etc.), BINARY values occupy 2 bytes in memory:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Signed BINARY}: Range -32,768 to 32,767 (using 2's complement representation)
\item \textbf{Unsigned BINARY}: Range 0 to 65,535 (unsigned magnitude)
\item \textbf{Storage}: Two bytes in little-endian or big-endian order (depending on backend)
\item \textbf{Operations}: Addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, bitwise operations
\item \textbf{Default type}: When no type is specified, variables default to BINARY SIGNED
\end{itemize}

BINARY type is the most efficient for general-purpose integer arithmetic, especially for loop counters, array indices, and mathematical computations.

\subsection{DECIMAL Type Details}

The DECIMAL type implements Binary-Coded Decimal (BCD), where each decimal digit (0-9) is encoded in a 4-bit nibble:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Format}: Two decimal digits per byte; e.g., byte 0x34 represents decimal 34
\item \textbf{Signed DECIMAL}: Sign nibble (C for +, D or B for -) at the most significant position
\item \textbf{Unsigned DECIMAL}: All nibbles contain digits 0-9
\item \textbf{Range}: 0-9999 unsigned, -9999 to +9999 signed (requires 2-4 bytes)
\item \textbf{Operations}: Add, subtract, multiply, divide via BCD arithmetic
\item \textbf{Precision}: Exactly 4 decimal digits; values beyond this truncate or error
\end{itemize}

DECIMAL type is valuable in business applications where decimal precision is critical and where BCD arithmetic hardware instructions are available on the target processor.

\subsection{DISPLAY Type Details}

The DISPLAY type stores numbers as ASCII character strings, where each digit is encoded as its ASCII value (0x30 for '0', 0x31 for '1', etc.):

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Format}: One ASCII byte per digit; "12345" stored as 5 bytes: 0x31 0x32 0x33 0x34 0x35
\item \textbf{Signed DISPLAY}: May include leading minus sign ('-')
\item \textbf{Flexibility}: Supports variable-length numbers within field width
\item \textbf{Range}: Limited by field width; no inherent numeric limit
\item \textbf{Operations}: Input/output (printing, parsing), string operations
\item \textbf{Use case}: User input validation, external data interchange, formatted output
\end{itemize}

DISPLAY type is often used for reading user input, as it allows direct ASCII parsing without binary conversion overhead.

\section{How BASIC Statements Map to EightBol IR}

Before diving into individual statement documentation, it's important to understand how EightBol BASIC statements map to the underlying intermediate representation (IR) used by the compiler:

\subsection{AST Node Types}

Each BASIC statement produces one or more AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) nodes:

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{BASIC Statement} & \textbf{AST Node Type} & \textbf{IR Purpose} \\
\hline
LET & MOVE & Variable assignment \\
PRINT & PRINT & Output operation \\
INPUT & INPUT & Input operation \\
IF/THEN/ELSE & IF & Conditional execution \\
FOR/NEXT & PERFORM & Loop with counter \\
WHILE/WEND & IF (loop) & Conditional loop \\
GOSUB & PERFORM & Subroutine call \\
RETURN & GOBACK & Subroutine return \\
GOTO & GOTO & Unconditional jump \\
STOP & STOP-RUN & Program termination \\
END & GOBACK (exit) & Program end \\
REM & (comment) & Documentation \\
DIM & ALLOCATE & Array allocation \\
READ & READ-DATA & Read from data block \\
DATA & DATA-BLOCK & Data definition \\
RESTORE & RESTORE & Reset read pointer \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Type Coercion and Conversion}

The EightBol compiler automatically inserts type coercion nodes when mixing numeric types:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY to DECIMAL}: Packs binary value into BCD using MOD/DIV operations
\item \textbf{DECIMAL to BINARY}: Unpacks BCD nibbles and accumulates to binary
\item \textbf{DISPLAY to BINARY}: Parses ASCII string character-by-character
\item \textbf{BINARY to DISPLAY}: Converts to ASCII using divide-by-10 algorithm
\end{itemize}

Type coercions are backend-specific; each processor has optimized paths for common conversions.

\subsection{Runtime vs. Compile-Time Behavior}

BASIC statements have both compile-time (static) and runtime (dynamic) behavior:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Compile-time}: Parser validates syntax, resolves labels, allocates variables
\item \textbf{Runtime}: Backend generates CPU-specific code that executes statements
\item \textbf{Optimization}: Redundant type conversions are eliminated; constants are folded
\item \textbf{Error handling}: Compile-time errors prevent code generation; runtime errors execute program-defined handlers
\end{itemize}



\section{1. LET Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{LET} statement assigns the result of an expression to a variable, making it the fundamental operation for variable initialization and modification.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
LET variable = expression
LET variable-name = numeric-expression
LET variable-name = string-expression
LET array-element = expression
LET variable = expression1 + expression2 + ... + expressionN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Keyword/Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
LET & Statement keyword (case-insensitive) \\
variable & Identifier for target variable (auto-normalized to PascalCase) \\
= & Assignment operator \\
expression & Arithmetic, string, or logical expression \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

When assigning numeric values, the source type is converted to the target variable's declared type:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY source → BINARY target}: Direct copy, no conversion
\item \textbf{DECIMAL source → DECIMAL target}: BCD-to-BCD transfer, preserving sign
\item \textbf{DISPLAY source → DISPLAY target}: ASCII string copy
\item \textbf{Mixed types}: Implicit conversion via intermediate representation
  \begin{itemize}
  \item BINARY → DECIMAL: Convert via unpacking BCD digits
  \item DECIMAL → BINARY: Convert via BCD accumulation
  \item DISPLAY → BINARY: Parse ASCII digits to binary value
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple Assignment with BINARY}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM Program: SIMPLE_ASSIGNMENT
20 LET X = 42
30 LET Y = X + 8
40 PRINT "Result: ", Y
50 END

Expected Output: Result: 50
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: DECIMAL Type Assignment (BCD Signed)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM Decimal assignment with sign
20 USAGE X IS DECIMAL SIGNED.
30 LET X = 1234
40 LET Y = X
50 PRINT "BCD Value: ", Y
55 REM Y now contains BCD-encoded 1234
60 END

Expected: Y = +1234 in BCD format
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Type Conversion - BINARY to DECIMAL}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM Convert binary to decimal
20 USAGE BIN IS BINARY SIGNED.
25 USAGE DEC IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
30 LET BIN = 255
40 LET DEC = BIN
50 PRINT "Binary 255 → Decimal: ", DEC
60 END

Expected: DEC holds BCD representation of 255
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: String Expression to DISPLAY}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM String assignment
20 USAGE NAME IS DISPLAY.
30 LET NAME = "ALICE"
40 PRINT NAME
50 END

Expected Output: ALICE
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Complex Expression with Overflow Handling}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM Complex expression
20 USAGE A IS BINARY SIGNED.
25 USAGE B IS BINARY SIGNED.
30 LET A = 32767
40 LET B = A + 1
45 REM B contains -32768 (signed 16-bit overflow)
50 PRINT "Overflow result: ", B
60 END

Expected: B = -32768 (wraparound on 16-bit signed)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Overflow on signed BINARY}: Wraps to negative boundary
\item \textbf{Overflow on unsigned BINARY}: Wraps to zero
\item \textbf{DECIMAL precision loss}: Values > 9999 truncate or error
\item \textbf{DISPLAY truncation}: Long strings may be truncated to field width
\item \textbf{Sign preservation}: Signed types maintain sign bit during conversion
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation Notes for LET}

The LET statement is translated to a MOVE node in the EightBol Abstract Syntax Tree. At compile time:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The parser recognizes the LET keyword and extracts the target variable and source expression
\item Variable names are normalized to PascalCase (e.g., `my_var` becomes `MyVar`)
\item The source expression is evaluated in a temporary if necessary
\item Type coercion is inserted if source and target types differ
\item For BINARY types, the move is a direct register-to-register or memory-to-memory transfer
\item For DECIMAL types, the move may include BCD validation and sign handling
\item For DISPLAY types, the move includes bounds checking and field width enforcement
\end{enumerate}

At runtime (code generation phase):

\begin{itemize}
\item For 6502 backend: Generates LDA/STA or LDX/STX sequences for BINARY; BCD instructions for DECIMAL
\item For Z80 backend: Generates LD r, (addr) or LD (addr), r sequences; BCD via DAA instruction
\item For ARM7 backend: Uses MOV and conditional code for type-specific operations
\item Overflow behavior depends on the backend's instruction set
\end{itemize}


\section{2. PRINT Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{PRINT} statement outputs expressions to standard output, supporting multiple items separated by commas.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
PRINT
PRINT expression
PRINT expression1, expression2, ..., expressionN
PRINT string-literal
PRINT variable-name
PRINT numeric-expression
PRINT "Text", variable, expression
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
PRINT & Statement keyword \\
expression & Numeric, string, or logical expression to output \\
, & Separator between multiple output items \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

Output formatting depends on the source numeric type:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY signed}: Decimal digits with optional minus sign
\item \textbf{BINARY unsigned}: Decimal digits only
\item \textbf{DECIMAL}: BCD digits decoded to ASCII decimal representation
\item \textbf{DISPLAY}: ASCII string printed as-is
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple Value Output}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 100
20 PRINT X
30 END

Output: 100
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: Multiple Items with Mixed Types}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET A = 42
20 LET B = 17
30 PRINT "Sum: ", A + B, " Average: ", (A + B) / 2
40 END

Output: Sum: 59 Average: 29
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: DECIMAL Output (BCD Conversion)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Price IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
20 LET Price = 1995
30 PRINT "Price: $", Price
40 END

Output: Price: $1995
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: Empty PRINT (Newline)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Line 1"
20 PRINT
30 PRINT "Line 3"
40 END

Output:
Line 1

Line 3
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Sign Handling in Output}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE NUM IS BINARY SIGNED.
20 LET NUM = -456
30 PRINT "Value: ", NUM
40 END

Output: Value: -456
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Newline behavior}: Empty PRINT outputs only newline
\item \textbf{No space between items}: Commas don't add spacing; use strings for formatting
\item \textbf{DECIMAL overflow}: Values > 9999 may display as corrupted
\item \textbf{String truncation}: Very long strings may overflow output buffer
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation Notes for PRINT}

The PRINT statement is translated to a PRINT node in the EightBol AST. At compile time:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The parser extracts the list of expressions to output
\item Each expression is analyzed for type (numeric or string)
\item Type-appropriate formatting instructions are inserted
\item String literals are stored in the program's string table and referenced by offset
\item A sequence of output primitives is generated
\end{enumerate}

At runtime (backend code generation):

\begin{itemize}
\item For BINARY signed: Convert to ASCII decimal with optional minus sign
\item For BINARY unsigned: Convert to ASCII decimal (unsigned)
\item For DECIMAL: Unpack BCD nibbles to ASCII and output with sign if needed
\item For DISPLAY: Output string directly with field width enforcement
\item Newline handling: Empty PRINT generates a carriage-return/line-feed sequence
\item Buffer management: Output goes to stdout or configured output device
\item Field width: Padding with spaces or zeros as required by format specification
\end{itemize}


\section{3. INPUT Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{INPUT} statement reads values from standard input into one or more variables, blocking until input is provided.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
INPUT variable
INPUT variable1, variable2, ..., variableN
INPUT array-element
INPUT var1, var2, array-element, var3
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
INPUT & Statement keyword \\
variable & Target variable identifier (auto-normalized to PascalCase) \\
, & Separator between multiple targets \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

Input values are parsed and converted to the target variable's numeric type:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY target}: Parse ASCII decimal to signed/unsigned binary
\item \textbf{DECIMAL target}: Parse ASCII to BCD encoding
\item \textbf{DISPLAY target}: Read as ASCII string directly
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Single Integer Input}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Enter a number: "
20 INPUT N
30 PRINT "You entered: ", N
40 END

User Input: 42
Output:
Enter a number: 
You entered: 42
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: Multiple Variable Input}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Enter three numbers: "
20 INPUT A, B, C
30 PRINT "Sum: ", A + B + C
40 END

User Input: 10
20
30
Output:
Enter three numbers: 
Sum: 60
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: DECIMAL Input with Sign}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Amount IS DECIMAL SIGNED.
20 PRINT "Enter amount: "
30 INPUT Amount
40 PRINT "Amount in BCD: ", Amount
50 END

User Input: -1234
Output shows Amount stored as signed BCD
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: Mixed Type Input}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Num IS BINARY SIGNED.
20 USAGE Text IS DISPLAY.
30 PRINT "Enter number and text: "
40 INPUT Num, Text
50 PRINT "Received: ", Num, " and ", Text
60 END

User Input:
99
HelloWorld
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Input with Validation Loop}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Age IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
20 LABEL InputLoop
30 PRINT "Enter age (0-120): "
40 INPUT Age
50 IF Age > 120 THEN GOTO ErrorMsg
60 GOTO InputDone
70 LABEL ErrorMsg
80 PRINT "Invalid age, try again"
90 GOTO InputLoop
100 LABEL InputDone
110 PRINT "Age accepted: ", Age
120 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Non-numeric input}: Conversion may fail or produce zero
\item \textbf{Overflow}: Input larger than target type wraps or truncates
\item \textbf{Negative UNSIGNED}: Undefined behavior; typically wraps to positive
\item \textbf{EOF}: May cause program termination or undefined behavior
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation Notes for INPUT}

The INPUT statement generates an INPUT node in the AST. At compile time:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Parser extracts the list of target variables
\item Type information for each variable is collected
\item Input handlers are generated for each target type
\item Error handling code is inserted if overflow is possible
\end{enumerate}

At runtime (backend code generation):

\begin{itemize}
\item For BINARY target: Read ASCII decimal string, convert to binary value
\item For DECIMAL target: Read ASCII decimal string, convert to BCD encoding
\item For DISPLAY target: Read ASCII string directly into field
\item Sign handling: Recognizes leading '+' or '-' for signed types
\item Whitespace: Leading whitespace typically skipped; trailing whitespace stops parsing
\item Multiple inputs: Each variable reads until whitespace/newline; continues to next variable
\item Buffer overflow: Long input strings may be truncated or cause runtime error
\item Non-numeric input: Zero-fill or error depending on backend
\end{itemize}


\section{4. IF...THEN...ELSE Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{IF...THEN...ELSE} statement conditionally executes one of two code paths based on a boolean condition.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
IF condition THEN statement
IF condition THEN statement ELSE statement
IF expression1 = expression2 THEN statement
IF expression1 < expression2 THEN statement ELSE statement
IF (condition1 AND condition2) THEN statement
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
IF & Conditional statement keyword \\
condition & Boolean expression (non-zero = true, zero = false) \\
THEN & Separator between condition and true branch \\
statement & Single statement to execute (no multi-statement blocks) \\
ELSE & Separator for false branch \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling in Conditions}

Conditions are evaluated to numeric values:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Relational operators} (=, <>, <, <=, >, >=) return 1 (true) or 0 (false)
\item \textbf{Logical operators} (AND, OR, NOT) combine boolean results
\item \textbf{BINARY comparisons}: Direct binary arithmetic comparison
\item \textbf{DECIMAL comparisons}: BCD-to-BCD comparison (unpacking as needed)
\item \textbf{DISPLAY comparisons}: ASCII lexicographic comparison
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple Comparison}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 10
20 IF X > 5 THEN PRINT "X is greater than 5"
30 END

Output: X is greater than 5
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: IF...THEN...ELSE with Numeric Types}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Score IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
20 LET Score = 75
30 IF Score >= 60 THEN PRINT "Pass" ELSE PRINT "Fail"
40 END

Output: Pass
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Compound Conditions with AND}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Age IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
15 USAGE HasLicense IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
20 LET Age = 25
25 LET HasLicense = 1
30 IF (Age >= 18) AND (HasLicense = 1) THEN PRINT "Can drive"
40 END

Output: Can drive
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: Nested IF (via repeated THEN statements)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Value IS BINARY SIGNED.
20 LET Value = -5
30 IF Value < 0 THEN PRINT "Negative"
40 IF Value = 0 THEN PRINT "Zero"
50 IF Value > 0 THEN PRINT "Positive"
60 END

Output: Negative
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: DECIMAL Comparison}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE PriceA IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
15 USAGE PriceB IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
20 LET PriceA = 1999
25 LET PriceB = 2500
30 IF PriceA < PriceB THEN PRINT "A is cheaper" ELSE PRINT "B is cheaper"
40 END

Output: A is cheaper
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Multiple THEN on one line}: Use ELSE or separate lines
\item \textbf{Non-zero falsy values}: Only zero is false; all non-zero is true
\item \textbf{Type mismatch in comparison}: Implicit conversion may occur
\item \textbf{String comparisons}: Lexicographic (ASCII) order, not numeric
\end{itemize}

\section{5. FOR...TO...STEP / NEXT Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{FOR...TO...STEP} loop iterates a specified number of times, incrementing a loop variable by a step value each iteration. The @code{NEXT} statement marks the end of the loop body.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
FOR variable = start TO end
  (statements)
NEXT [variable]

FOR variable = start TO end STEP increment
  (statements)
NEXT [variable]

FOR I = 1 TO 10 STEP 2
  (loop body)
NEXT I
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
FOR & Loop initiation keyword \\
variable & Loop counter variable \\
= & Assignment in FOR context \\
start & Initial value (numeric expression) \\
TO & Keyword separating start from end \\
end & Final value (numeric expression, inclusive) \\
STEP & Optional keyword for non-unit increment \\
increment & Step size (default 1 if STEP omitted) \\
NEXT & Loop footer keyword (variable optional) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling in FOR Loops}

Loop counters and bounds support all numeric types:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY loops}: Counter increments as signed/unsigned binary
\item \textbf{DECIMAL loops}: Counter increments in BCD; step converted to BCD
\item \textbf{DISPLAY loops}: String-based counters; increment as ASCII digits
\item \textbf{Type mixing}: start, end, and STEP may be different types; conversions applied
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple FOR Loop (BINARY Default)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 FOR I = 1 TO 5
20   PRINT I
30 NEXT I
40 END

Output:
1
2
3
4
5
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: FOR Loop with STEP}

\begin{verbatim}
10 FOR I = 0 TO 20 STEP 5
20   PRINT I
30 NEXT I
40 END

Output:
0
5
10
15
20
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Descending FOR Loop (Negative STEP)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 FOR I = 10 TO 1 STEP -1
20   PRINT I
30 NEXT I
40 END

Output:
10
9
8
...
2
1
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: DECIMAL Loop Counter}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Counter IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
20 USAGE Result IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
30 FOR Counter = 100 TO 105
40   LET Result = Counter
50   PRINT Result
60 NEXT Counter
70 END

Output: 100, 101, 102, 103, 104, 105 (in BCD format)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Nested FOR Loops}

\begin{verbatim}
10 FOR I = 1 TO 3
20   FOR J = 1 TO 2
30     PRINT I, " ", J
40   NEXT J
50 NEXT I
60 END

Output:
1 1
1 2
2 1
2 2
3 1
3 2
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Step = 0}: Infinite loop (no exit condition met)
\item \textbf{start > end with positive STEP}: Loop never executes
\item \textbf{Counter modification inside loop}: Affects loop iteration count
\item \textbf{Loop variable type mismatch}: Implicit conversion on entry/exit
\item \textbf{Float/fractional step}: Truncated to integer
\end{itemize}

\section{6. WHILE Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{WHILE} statement repeatedly executes code while a condition remains true, evaluating the condition at the top of each iteration.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
WHILE condition
  statement
  (more statements)
WEND
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
WHILE & Loop initiation keyword \\
condition & Boolean condition (non-zero = continue, zero = exit) \\
statement & Code to repeat \\
WEND & Loop termination keyword (end-while) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling in WHILE}

Conditions evaluated using the same rules as @code{IF}:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY comparison}: Direct arithmetic evaluation
\item \textbf{DECIMAL comparison}: BCD decoded for evaluation
\item \textbf{DISPLAY comparison}: Lexicographic string comparison
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple WHILE Loop}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 1
20 WHILE X <= 5
30   PRINT X
40   LET X = X + 1
50 WEND
60 END

Output:
1
2
3
4
5
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: WHILE with Sentinel Value}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET N = 0
20 WHILE N <> -1
30   PRINT "Enter number (-1 to quit): "
40   INPUT N
50   IF N <> -1 THEN PRINT "You entered: ", N
60 WEND
70 PRINT "Goodbye"
80 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: WHILE with DECIMAL Counter}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE Count IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
20 LET Count = 0
30 WHILE Count < 10
40   PRINT Count
50   LET Count = Count + 1
60 WEND
70 END

Output: 0-9 in BCD format
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: WHILE with Compound Condition}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE X IS BINARY SIGNED.
15 USAGE Y IS BINARY SIGNED.
20 LET X = 5
25 LET Y = 10
30 WHILE (X > 0) AND (Y > 0)
40   PRINT X, ", ", Y
50   LET X = X - 1
60   LET Y = Y - 2
70 WEND
80 END

Output:
5, 10
4, 8
3, 6
2, 4
1, 2
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: WHILE with Early Exit via GOTO}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 0
20 WHILE X < 100
30   IF X = 50 THEN GOTO ExitLoop
40   LET X = X + 1
50 WEND
60 LABEL ExitLoop
70 PRINT "Final X: ", X
80 END

Output: Final X: 50
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Condition initially false}: Loop never executes
\item \textbf{Infinite loop}: Condition always true; requires GOTO or external interrupt
\item \textbf{Type conversion in condition}: May affect loop termination
\item \textbf{Counter not modified}: Leads to infinite loop
\end{itemize}

\section{7. GOSUB Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{GOSUB} statement transfers control to a subroutine at a specified label, with automatic return via @code{RETURN}.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
GOSUB target
GOSUB PROCEDURE-NAME
GOSUB line-number
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
GOSUB & Subroutine call keyword \\
target & Label name or line number where subroutine starts \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

GOSUB itself is control-flow; numeric handling occurs in called code:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Parameter passing}: Via global variables (BASIC has no local scoping)
\item \textbf{Return values}: Similarly via global variables
\item \textbf{Subroutine modification of types}: Affects caller's variables
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple Subroutine Call}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Main program"
20 GOSUB 100
30 PRINT "Back in main"
40 END
100 REM Subroutine starts here
110 PRINT "In subroutine"
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: Subroutine with Numeric Calculation}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 5
20 LET Y = 3
30 GOSUB CalcSum
40 PRINT "Sum: ", Result
50 END
100 LABEL CalcSum
110 LET Result = X + Y
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Nested GOSUB (Subroutine Calling Subroutine)}

\begin{verbatim}
10 GOSUB Sub1
20 END
100 LABEL Sub1
110 PRINT "In Sub1"
120 GOSUB Sub2
130 PRINT "Back in Sub1"
140 RETURN
200 LABEL Sub2
210 PRINT "In Sub2"
220 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: GOSUB with Type Conversion}

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE BinVal IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
20 USAGE DecVal IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
30 LET BinVal = 255
40 GOSUB Convert
50 PRINT "Decimal result: ", DecVal
60 END
100 LABEL Convert
110 LET DecVal = BinVal
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: GOSUB with Multiple Return Paths}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET N = 5
20 GOSUB CheckRange
30 END
100 LABEL CheckRange
110 IF N < 0 THEN PRINT "Negative" ELSE PRINT "Non-negative"
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RETURN without GOSUB}: Undefined behavior (may halt or return to OS)
\item \textbf{Deep nesting}: Stack overflow on small systems
\item \textbf{GOSUB into THEN branch}: May skip initialization code
\item \textbf{Line number vs. label}: Line numbers may be dynamically assigned
\end{itemize}

\section{8. RETURN Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{RETURN} statement terminates the current subroutine and transfers control back to the instruction following the most recent @code{GOSUB}.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Keyword} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
RETURN & Subroutine exit keyword (no parameters) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

@code{RETURN} performs no numeric operations; return values are passed via global variables.

\subsection{Example 1: Basic Subroutine Return}

\begin{verbatim}
10 GOSUB MySub
20 PRINT "After subroutine"
30 END
100 LABEL MySub
110 PRINT "Inside subroutine"
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: RETURN with Computed Result}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET A = 10
20 GOSUB Double
30 PRINT "Doubled: ", A
40 END
100 LABEL Double
110 LET A = A * 2
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Conditional RETURN}

\begin{verbatim}
10 GOSUB Validate
20 PRINT "Validation passed"
30 END
100 LABEL Validate
110 IF BadCondition THEN RETURN
120 PRINT "Processing..."
130 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: RETURN from Multiple Code Paths}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET Mode = 2
20 GOSUB ProcessMode
30 END
100 LABEL ProcessMode
110 IF Mode = 1 THEN PRINT "Mode 1" ELSE PRINT "Other"
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: RETURN with Type-Converted Result}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET Result = 0
20 GOSUB CalcDecimal
30 PRINT "Result type: DECIMAL"
40 END
100 LABEL CalcDecimal
110 USAGE TempDec IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
120 LET TempDec = 9999
130 LET Result = TempDec
140 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RETURN without GOSUB}: Halts program or returns to OS
\item \textbf{Multiple RETURN statements}: All valid within subroutine
\item \textbf{RETURN in main program}: Terminates program
\end{itemize}

\section{9. GOTO Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{GOTO} statement transfers control unconditionally to a labeled line or line number, creating an unconditional branch.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
GOTO label
GOTO line-number
GOTO "quoted-label"
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
GOTO & Unconditional branch keyword \\
label & Target label name (auto-normalized to PascalCase) \\
line-number & Target line number (generates BASIC\_Lnnnnnn label) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

@code{GOTO} performs no computation; it's purely control-flow.

\subsection{Example 1: Simple Forward GOTO}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Before jump"
20 GOTO Skip
30 PRINT "This is skipped"
40 LABEL Skip
50 PRINT "After jump"
60 END

Output:
Before jump
After jump
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: Backward GOTO (Loop)**

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 1
20 LABEL Loop
30 PRINT X
40 LET X = X + 1
50 IF X <= 5 THEN GOTO Loop
60 PRINT "Done"
70 END

Output:
1
2
3
4
5
Done
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: GOTO to Error Handler}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET Value = -5
20 IF Value < 0 THEN GOTO ErrorHandler
30 PRINT "Value is valid: ", Value
40 GOTO End
50 LABEL ErrorHandler
60 PRINT "Error: negative value"
70 LABEL End
80 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: GOTO with Line Number}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Start"
20 GOTO 50
30 PRINT "Skipped"
40 PRINT "Also skipped"
50 PRINT "End"
60 END

Output:
Start
End
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Multiple GOTO Targets}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET Mode = 2
20 IF Mode = 1 THEN GOTO ModeOne
30 IF Mode = 2 THEN GOTO ModeTwo
40 GOTO ModeDefault
50 LABEL ModeOne
60 PRINT "Mode 1 processing"
70 GOTO Done
80 LABEL ModeTwo
90 PRINT "Mode 2 processing"
100 GOTO Done
110 LABEL ModeDefault
120 PRINT "Default mode"
130 LABEL Done
140 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{GOTO undefined label}: Compiler error or runtime fault
\item \textbf{Forward GOTO}: All valid; creates label references
\item \textbf{Infinite loops}: Backward GOTO with no exit condition
\item \textbf{GOTO into IF branch}: May skip variable initialization
\end{itemize}

\section{10. STOP Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{STOP} statement terminates program execution with an optional code/message.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
STOP
STOP "message"
STOP code-string
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
STOP & Program termination keyword \\
"message" & Optional string literal (exit code/message) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

@code{STOP} takes a string parameter (no numeric type conversion).

\subsection{Example 1: Simple STOP}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Processing..."
20 STOP
30 PRINT "This never executes"
40 END

Output: Processing...
Program terminates
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: STOP with Message}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 100
20 PRINT "X = ", X
30 STOP "NORMAL_EXIT"
40 END

Output:
X = 100
Program terminates with code NORMAL_EXIT
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Conditional STOP}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET N = 0
20 INPUT "Enter positive number: ", N
30 IF N <= 0 THEN STOP "ERROR_NEGATIVE_VALUE"
40 PRINT "Value accepted: ", N
50 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: STOP in Error Handler}

\begin{verbatim}
10 GOSUB Validate
20 PRINT "Validation passed"
30 END
100 LABEL Validate
110 IF BadData THEN STOP "VALIDATION_FAILED"
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: STOP vs. END}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Line 1"
20 STOP "EARLY_EXIT"
30 PRINT "Line 3"
40 END

Output: Line 1
Program exits at STOP
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{STOP with no END}: Program terminates at STOP
\item \textbf{Exit code length}: May be truncated on some systems
\item \textbf{Cleanup}: No cleanup code runs after STOP
\end{itemize}

\section{11. END Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{END} statement marks the end of the main program and signals normal termination.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Keyword} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
END & Program termination keyword (no parameters) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

@code{END} performs no computations.

\subsection{Example 1: Simple END**

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Hello, World!"
20 END

Output: Hello, World!
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: END After Main Logic}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 5
20 LET Y = X * 2
30 PRINT "Result: ", Y
40 END

Output: Result: 10
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: END vs. STOP}

\begin{verbatim}
10 PRINT "Before STOP"
20 STOP "EARLY"
30 PRINT "After STOP (not reached)"
40 END

Output: Before STOP
(Program exits at STOP; END not reached)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: Multiple Subroutines with END}

\begin{verbatim}
10 GOSUB Sub1
20 PRINT "Main continues"
30 END
100 LABEL Sub1
110 PRINT "In Sub1"
120 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Program Flow to END}

\begin{verbatim}
10 LABEL Start
20 PRINT "Line 1"
30 GOTO End
40 PRINT "Skipped"
50 LABEL End
60 END

Output: Line 1
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Missing END}: Program may continue past intended end
\item \textbf{Multiple END statements}: Only first one terminates (subsequent unreachable)
\item \textbf{Subroutine after END}: Unreachable unless GOSUB skips END
\end{itemize}

\section{12. REM Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{REM} statement inserts a comment that is ignored during compilation and execution.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
REM This is a comment
100 REM Comment after line number
REM Can span to end of line
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Keyword} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
REM & Comment keyword (rest of line ignored) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

@code{REM} is pre-processing; no numeric operations occur.

\subsection{Example 1: Simple Comment}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM This program calculates factorials
20 LET X = 5
30 PRINT X
40 END

Output: 5
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: Multi-line Comments}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM ====================================
20 REM Program: Calculate Average
25 REM Input: Three integers
30 REM Output: Average (integer)
35 REM ====================================
40 INPUT A, B, C
50 PRINT (A + B + C) / 3
60 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Inline Comments (After Code)**

\begin{verbatim}
10 LET X = 10 REM Initialize X to 10
20 LET Y = X * 2 REM Double X
30 PRINT Y REM Display Y
40 END

Output: 20
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: Comment Before Assignment}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM Set up loop variables
20 LET I = 1
30 REM Main loop
40 LABEL LoopStart
50 PRINT I
60 LET I = I + 1
70 IF I <= 5 THEN GOTO LoopStart
80 END
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Documentation Comments}

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM SUBROUTINE: AddNumbers
20 REM Input: A, B (global variables)
25 REM Output: Result (global variable)
30 REM ---
40 LET Result = A + B
50 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{REM with no text}: Empty comment (valid)
\item \textbf{Special characters in REM}: All allowed; not parsed
\item \textbf{Very long REM}: May exceed line buffer
\end{itemize}

\section{13. DIM Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{DIM} statement declares arrays and their dimensions, allocating storage for elements.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
DIM array-name(size)
DIM array-name(rows, cols)
DIM array-name(d1, d2, ..., dN)
DIM A(10), B(5, 5), C(3, 4, 5)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
DIM & Array declaration keyword \\
array-name & Identifier for array variable \\
(size) & Single or comma-separated dimensions \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

Arrays store the declared element type; element type inferred from usage or context:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY arrays}: Elements are binary integers
\item \textbf{DECIMAL arrays}: Elements are BCD encoded
\item \textbf{DISPLAY arrays}: Elements are strings
\item \textbf{Type of dimensions}: Numeric expressions; typically BINARY
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Single-Dimension Array}

\begin{verbatim}
10 DIM Numbers(10)
20 FOR I = 1 TO 10
30   LET Numbers(I) = I * 10
40 NEXT I
50 PRINT Numbers(5)
60 END

Output: 50
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: Two-Dimensional Array**

\begin{verbatim}
10 DIM Matrix(3, 3)
20 FOR I = 1 TO 3
30   FOR J = 1 TO 3
40     LET Matrix(I, J) = I + J
50   NEXT J
60 NEXT I
70 PRINT Matrix(2, 2)
80 END

Output: 4
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: DECIMAL Array**

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE PriceList IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
20 DIM PriceList(5)
30 LET PriceList(1) = 9999
40 LET PriceList(2) = 1500
50 PRINT PriceList(1)
60 END

Output: 9999 (BCD format)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: Multiple Array Declarations**

\begin{verbatim}
10 DIM Names(5), Ages(5), Scores(5)
20 LET Names(1) = "Alice"
30 LET Ages(1) = 25
40 LET Scores(1) = 95
50 PRINT Names(1), " age ", Ages(1)
60 END

Output: Alice age 25
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: 3D Array**

\begin{verbatim}
10 DIM Cube(2, 2, 2)
20 LET Cube(1, 1, 1) = 100
30 LET Cube(2, 2, 2) = 999
40 PRINT Cube(2, 2, 2)
50 END

Output: 999
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Array size = 0}: Typically error or creates empty array
\item \textbf{Dimension overflow}: Accessing beyond array bounds is undefined
\item \textbf{Negative indices}: Not supported; typically cause error
\item \textbf{DIM inside loop}: Creates array on each iteration (inefficient)
\end{itemize}

\section{14. READ Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{READ} statement retrieves values from @code{DATA} statements sequentially, advancing a pointer through declared data.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
READ variable
READ var1, var2, ..., varN
READ array-element
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
READ & Data retrieval keyword \\
variable & Target variable for data value \\
, & Separator between multiple targets \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

READ converts data values to target variable types:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY target}: Parse data value as binary integer
\item \textbf{DECIMAL target}: Convert/store as BCD
\item \textbf{DISPLAY target}: Store as ASCII string
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple READ from DATA}

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ X
20 PRINT "Value: ", X
30 END
1000 DATA 42
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: READ Multiple Values}

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ A, B, C
20 PRINT "Sum: ", A + B + C
30 END
1000 DATA 10, 20, 30
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: READ into Array**

\begin{verbatim}
10 DIM Numbers(5)
20 FOR I = 1 TO 5
30   READ Numbers(I)
40 NEXT I
50 FOR I = 1 TO 5
60   PRINT Numbers(I)
70 NEXT I
80 END
1000 DATA 11, 22, 33, 44, 55
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: READ with Type Conversion**

\begin{verbatim}
10 USAGE BinVal IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
20 USAGE DecVal IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
30 READ BinVal
40 READ DecVal
50 PRINT "Binary: ", BinVal, " Decimal: ", DecVal
60 END
1000 DATA 255, 9999
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Multiple READ Blocks**

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ X, Y
20 PRINT "First pair: ", X, Y
30 READ X, Y
40 PRINT "Second pair: ", X, Y
50 END
1000 DATA 1, 2, 3, 4
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{READ with no DATA}: May cause error or read from uninitialized memory
\item \textbf{More READ than DATA}: May read garbage or cause segfault
\item \textbf{Type mismatch in DATA}: Conversion may fail or produce unexpected values
\end{itemize}

\section{15. DATA Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{DATA} statement declares constant values that are read sequentially by @code{READ} statements.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
DATA value1, value2, ..., valueN
DATA 100, 200, 300
DATA "string1", "string2"
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
DATA & Data declaration keyword \\
value & Numeric literal or string literal \\
, & Separator between values \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

DATA values are stored as literals; type determined by format:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Numeric literal}: Stored as binary (converted to target type by READ)
\item \textbf{String literal}: Stored as ASCII string
\item \textbf{Mixed types}: Allowed within same DATA statement
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Simple DATA**

\begin{verbatim}
100 DATA 10, 20, 30, 40, 50
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: String DATA**

\begin{verbatim}
200 DATA "Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Mixed DATA (Multiple Statements)**

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ Name
20 READ Age
30 PRINT Name, " is ", Age, " years old"
40 END
1000 DATA "Alice"
1010 DATA 25
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: DATA with Numeric Formats**

\begin{verbatim}
100 DATA 100, 0xFF, 0o77, 0b1010
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: Large DATA Block**

\begin{verbatim}
10 DIM Scores(10)
20 FOR I = 1 TO 10
30   READ Scores(I)
40 NEXT I
50 END
1000 DATA 95, 87, 92, 88, 91
1010 DATA 85, 90, 89, 93, 96
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{DATA in middle of code}: Execution continues; data read by READ
\item \textbf{DATA ordering}: Read sequentially from first DATA encountered
\item \textbf{Duplicate DATA**: All collected; no deduplication
\end{itemize}

\section{16. RESTORE Statement}

\subsection{Purpose}
The @code{RESTORE} statement resets the @code{READ} pointer to the beginning of @code{DATA} statements, allowing re-reading of data.

\subsection{Complete Syntax}

\begin{verbatim}
RESTORE
RESTORE line-number
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Keywords and Operands}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Explanation} \\
\hline
RESTORE & Data pointer reset keyword \\
line-number & Optional; specific DATA line to restore to (if supported) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Numeric Type Handling}

@code{RESTORE} is pure control; no numeric operations.

\subsection{Example 1: Simple RESTORE}

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ X
20 PRINT "First read: ", X
30 RESTORE
40 READ Y
50 PRINT "Re-read: ", Y
60 END
1000 DATA 42
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 2: RESTORE and Loop**

\begin{verbatim}
10 FOR Pass = 1 TO 2
20   RESTORE
30   FOR I = 1 TO 3
40     READ Value
50     PRINT "Pass ", Pass, ": ", Value
60   NEXT I
70 NEXT Pass
80 END
1000 DATA 10, 20, 30
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 3: Multiple RESTORE Calls**

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ A, B
20 PRINT A, B
30 RESTORE
40 READ X, Y, Z
50 PRINT X, Y
60 END
1000 DATA 1, 2, 3
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 4: RESTORE in Subroutine**

\begin{verbatim}
10 GOSUB ReadData
20 GOSUB ReadData
30 END
100 LABEL ReadData
110 RESTORE
120 READ X
130 PRINT X
140 RETURN
1000 DATA 99
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Example 5: RESTORE with Partial Re-read**

\begin{verbatim}
10 READ A, B, C
20 PRINT "First: ", A, B, C
30 RESTORE
40 READ X
50 PRINT "After restore: ", X
60 END
1000 DATA 10, 20, 30
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Edge Cases}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RESTORE with no DATA}: No effect (or error)
\item \textbf{RESTORE line-number}: May not be supported; behavior varies
\item \textbf{Multiple RESTORE calls}: Each resets to beginning
\end{itemize}

\section{Numeric Type Reference Summary}

\subsection{BINARY Type}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Signed}: 2's complement, range -32768 to 32767 (16-bit)
\item \textbf{Unsigned}: 0 to 65535 (16-bit)
\item \textbf{Storage}: Native machine representation
\item \textbf{Literals}: Decimal, hex (&H), octal (&O), binary (&B)
\item \textbf{Default type}: Used when no type specified
\end{itemize}

\subsection{DECIMAL Type (BCD)}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Format}: 4 digits per byte (2 nibbles = 1 BCD digit)
\item \textbf{Signed}: Sign nibble (C=+, D/B=-) at high end
\item \textbf{Unsigned}: 0-9 in all nibbles
\item \textbf{Range}: 0-9999 (unsigned), -9999 to +9999 (signed)
\item \textbf{Conversions}: Automatic between BINARY and DECIMAL
\end{itemize}

\subsection{DISPLAY Type}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Format}: ASCII character string
\item \textbf{Encoding}: Each digit as ASCII byte (0x30-0x39 for 0-9)
\item \textbf{Range}: Limited by field width
\item \textbf{String operations}: Supported for concatenation
\item \textbf{Input}: Parsed from user input as digits
\end{itemize}

\section{Type Conversion Reference}

\subsection{Conversion Hierarchy}

\begin{verbatim}
BINARY ← → DECIMAL ← → DISPLAY
        ↘        ↙
           String Operations
\end{verbatim}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY to DECIMAL}: Pack into BCD, handling sign
\item \textbf{DECIMAL to BINARY}: Unpack BCD to binary, preserving sign
\item \textbf{BINARY to DISPLAY}: Convert to ASCII digit string
\item \textbf{DISPLAY to BINARY}: Parse ASCII to binary integer
\item \textbf{DECIMAL to DISPLAY}: Unpack BCD to ASCII string
\item \textbf{DISPLAY to DECIMAL}: Parse ASCII to BCD
\end{itemize}

\section{Statement Interaction Matrix}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Statement Pair} & \textbf{Interaction} \\
\hline
FOR/NEXT & Required pair; NEXT closes FOR \\
WHILE/WEND & Required pair; WEND closes WHILE \\
GOSUB/RETURN & Call/return; RETURN to instruction after GOSUB \\
IF/THEN & THEN executes if condition true \\
IF/ELSE & ELSE executes if condition false \\
GOTO/Label & Unconditional jump to label \\
READ/DATA & Sequential read from DATA block \\
RESTORE/DATA & Reset read pointer to beginning \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Edge Case Summary}

\subsection{Overflow Handling}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY signed}: Wraps to negative (2's complement)
\item \textbf{BINARY unsigned}: Wraps to zero
\item \textbf{DECIMAL}: Truncates or errors > 9999
\item \textbf{Type conversion}: May lose precision
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Scope and Lifetime}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Global variables}: Shared across main program and subroutines
\item \textbf{Local storage}: Not supported (all variables global)
\item \textbf{Array persistence}: Retained across subroutine calls
\item \textbf{Parameter passing}: Via global variable assignment
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Numeric Precision}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Integer-only}: No floating-point types
\item \textbf{BINARY precision}: 16-bit (65536 distinct values)
\item \textbf{DECIMAL precision}: 4 digits per 2 bytes
\item \textbf{DISPLAY precision}: String length limited
\end{itemize}

\section{Comprehensive Examples: Multi-Statement Programs}

\subsection{Example 1: Complete Factorial Calculator}

This program demonstrates LET, INPUT, FOR/NEXT, PRINT, IF/THEN, and type handling:

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM FACTORIAL CALCULATOR
15 REM Calculates N! where N is user input
20 PRINT "Factorial Calculator"
30 PRINT "Enter a number (1-20): "
40 INPUT N
50 IF N < 1 THEN GOTO ErrorInput
60 IF N > 20 THEN GOTO ErrorInput
70 GOSUB CalcFactorial
80 PRINT "Factorial of ", N, " = ", Result
90 STOP "SUCCESS"
100 LABEL ErrorInput
110 PRINT "Invalid input"
120 STOP "ERROR_INVALID"
200 LABEL CalcFactorial
210 LET Result = 1
220 FOR I = 2 TO N
230   LET Result = Result * I
240 NEXT I
250 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

This example showcases:
\begin{itemize}
\item REM for documentation
\item INPUT for user interaction
\item IF/THEN for validation
\item GOSUB/RETURN for code reuse
\item FOR/NEXT for iteration
\item Numeric computation with type preservation
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 2: Array Processing with Type Conversion}

This program demonstrates DIM, READ, DATA, RESTORE, type conversions, and control flow:

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM PROCESS SALES DATA
15 REM Read prices (DECIMAL), calculate total (BINARY)
20 DIM Prices(5), Quantities(5)
30 USAGE Total IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
40 USAGE DecSum IS DECIMAL UNSIGNED.
50 PRINT "Loading inventory data..."
60 RESTORE
70 FOR I = 1 TO 5
80   READ Prices(I), Quantities(I)
90 NEXT I
100 PRINT "Prices loaded"
110 PRINT
120 GOSUB CalculateTotal
130 PRINT "Total inventory value: ", Total
140 PRINT "In BCD format: ", DecSum
150 END
200 LABEL CalculateTotal
210 LET Total = 0
220 LET DecSum = 0
230 FOR I = 1 TO 5
240   LET LineTotal = Prices(I) * Quantities(I)
250   LET Total = Total + LineTotal
260 NEXT I
270 LET DecSum = Total
280 REM Convert BINARY total to DECIMAL for display
290 RETURN
1000 DATA 1500, 10
1010 DATA 2000, 5
1020 DATA 999, 20
1030 DATA 750, 15
1040 DATA 3500, 2
\end{verbatim}

This demonstrates:
\begin{itemize}
\item DIM for multi-element storage
\item READ/DATA for initialization
\item RESTORE for pointer management
\item Type conversion (BINARY to DECIMAL)
\item GOSUB for subroutine organization
\item Nested loops and accumulation
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 3: Menu-Driven Program with GOTO}

This example uses GOTO for menu navigation and conditional branching:

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM MENU-DRIVEN PROGRAM
20 LABEL MainMenu
30 PRINT "=== MAIN MENU ==="
40 PRINT "1. Add Numbers"
50 PRINT "2. Multiply Numbers"
60 PRINT "3. Exit"
70 PRINT "Choose (1-3): "
80 INPUT Choice
90 IF Choice = 1 THEN GOTO AddMenu
100 IF Choice = 2 THEN GOTO MultiplyMenu
110 IF Choice = 3 THEN GOTO Exit
120 PRINT "Invalid choice"
130 GOTO MainMenu
140 LABEL AddMenu
150 PRINT "Enter two numbers: "
160 INPUT X, Y
170 PRINT "Sum: ", X + Y
180 GOTO MainMenu
190 LABEL MultiplyMenu
200 PRINT "Enter two numbers: "
210 INPUT X, Y
220 PRINT "Product: ", X * Y
230 GOTO MainMenu
240 LABEL Exit
250 PRINT "Goodbye"
260 END
\end{verbatim}

This demonstrates:
\begin{itemize}
\item GOTO for loop-back control
\item Conditional GOTO for branching
\item IF/THEN with GOTO target
\item Menu structure design
\item Multiple code paths
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 4: Data Validation with WHILE Loop}

This example uses WHILE/WEND for input validation:

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM DATA VALIDATION LOOP
20 USAGE InputValue IS BINARY SIGNED.
30 USAGE IsValid IS BINARY UNSIGNED.
40 PRINT "Enter values between -100 and 100"
50 LABEL EntryLoop
60 LET IsValid = 0
70 WHILE IsValid = 0
80   PRINT "Enter a number: "
90   INPUT InputValue
100  IF InputValue < -100 THEN GOTO CheckHigh
110  IF InputValue > 100 THEN GOTO CheckFail
120  LET IsValid = 1
130  GOTO LoopEnd
140  LABEL CheckHigh
150  IF InputValue >= -100 THEN LET IsValid = 1
160  LABEL CheckFail
170  IF IsValid = 0 THEN PRINT "Out of range, try again"
180  LABEL LoopEnd
190 WEND
200 PRINT "Accepted: ", InputValue
210 END
\end{verbatim}

This demonstrates:
\begin{itemize}
\item WHILE/WEND for conditional looping
\item Compound validation conditions
\item Control variable management
\item Type declarations (SIGNED, UNSIGNED)
\item Multiple exit paths
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 5: Matrix Operations (2D Arrays)}

This example uses DIM for 2D arrays and nested FOR loops:

\begin{verbatim}
10 REM MATRIX ADDITION
20 DIM MatrixA(3, 3), MatrixB(3, 3), Result(3, 3)
30 PRINT "Initialize matrices..."
40 GOSUB InitMatrices
50 GOSUB AddMatrices
60 GOSUB PrintResult
70 END
100 LABEL InitMatrices
110 FOR I = 1 TO 3
120   FOR J = 1 TO 3
130     LET MatrixA(I, J) = I + J
140     LET MatrixB(I, J) = I * J
150   NEXT J
160 NEXT I
170 RETURN
200 LABEL AddMatrices
210 FOR I = 1 TO 3
220   FOR J = 1 TO 3
230     LET Result(I, J) = MatrixA(I, J) + MatrixB(I, J)
240   NEXT J
250 NEXT I
260 RETURN
300 LABEL PrintResult
310 PRINT "Result Matrix:"
320 FOR I = 1 TO 3
330   FOR J = 1 TO 3
340     PRINT Result(I, J), " "
350   NEXT J
360   PRINT
370 NEXT I
380 RETURN
\end{verbatim}

This demonstrates:
\begin{itemize}
\item Multi-dimensional arrays (3D structure)
\item Nested FOR/NEXT loops (up to 3 levels)
\item Subroutine organization with GOSUB/RETURN
\item Matrix-style indexing and operations
\item Iterative display of results
\end{itemize}

\section{Common Patterns and Best Practices}

\subsection{Loop Structure Pattern}

BASIC programs typically use one of two loop patterns:

\textbf{FOR/NEXT (count-based)}:
\begin{verbatim}
FOR I = Start TO End STEP S
  (process I)
NEXT I
\end{verbatim}

\textbf{WHILE/WEND (condition-based)}:
\begin{verbatim}
WHILE Condition
  (process)
  (update condition variable)
WEND
\end{verbatim}

The FOR/NEXT loop is preferred when the iteration count is known; WHILE/WEND for event-driven or sentinel-based loops.

\subsection{Subroutine Organization Pattern}

Subroutines are organized with clear entry and exit:

\begin{verbatim}
LABEL SubroutineName
REM input parameters in global vars (InVar1, InVar2, ...)
REM output results in global vars (OutVar1, OutVar2, ...)
(process)
RETURN
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Type Safety Pattern}

Declare all variables and their types explicitly:

\begin{verbatim}
USAGE VarName IS NUMERIC-TYPE QUALIFIER.
USAGE ArrayName(Size) IS NUMERIC-TYPE.
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Error Handling Pattern}

Use IF/THEN and GOTO for error conditions:

\begin{verbatim}
IF ErrorCondition THEN GOTO ErrorHandler
(normal processing)
GOTO Done
LABEL ErrorHandler
PRINT "Error message"
STOP "ERROR_CODE"
LABEL Done
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Data Initialization Pattern}

Use READ/DATA for static initialization:

\begin{verbatim}
RESTORE
FOR I = 1 TO Count
  READ Array(I)
NEXT I
LABEL DataStart
DATA value1, value2, ...
\end{verbatim}

\section{Numeric Type Decision Tree}

When choosing a numeric type for variables:

\begin{verbatim}
Does your value need decimals?
  ├─ No, all integers?
  │  ├─ Can it be negative? (e.g., balance, temperature)
  │  │  ├─ Yes → BINARY SIGNED (recommended for most)
  │  │  └─ No → BINARY UNSIGNED (for counts, indices)
  │  └─ Can it have exactly 4 digits? (e.g., price, BCD sensor)
  │     ├─ Yes → DECIMAL UNSIGNED (or SIGNED for +/-)
  │     └─ No → BINARY (convert to/from DECIMAL as needed)
  └─ Yes, need decimals?
     └─ Use DISPLAY as workaround (store "12.34" as string)
\end{verbatim}

\section{Migration Guide: From Other BASIC Dialects}

\subsection{From Microsoft BASIC}

EightBol BASIC differs from classic Microsoft BASIC in:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{PRINT formatting}: No PRINT@, TAB, or SPC; use string concatenation
\item \textbf{REDim}: Not supported; declare all arrays with DIM upfront
\item \textbf{Floating-point}: Not supported; use BINARY for integer arithmetic
\item \textbf{Built-in functions}: Limited set; see separate functions reference
\item \textbf{Modules}: Not supported; organize code with subroutines
\end{itemize}

\subsection{From Commodore BASIC}

Migration from Commodore BASIC requires:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{POKE/PEEK}: No direct memory access; use data structures
\item \textbf{Graphics}: No built-in graphics; use backend-specific extensions
\item \textbf{Color}: DISPLAY type for ANSI sequences; not native support
\item \textbf{Floating-point}: Convert algorithms to use BINARY integers
\end{itemize}

\subsection{From Dartmouth BASIC}

EightBol implements a Dartmouth-compatible subset:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Supported}: LET, IF/THEN/ELSE, FOR/NEXT, GOSUB/RETURN, PRINT, INPUT, READ/DATA, RESTORE
\item \textbf{Not supported}: String functions (beyond literals), DEF FN, END SUB
\item \textbf{Extensions}: DECIMAL, BINARY, DISPLAY types; WHILE/WEND; GOTO line-number
\end{itemize}

\section{Performance Considerations}

\subsection{Numeric Type Performance}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{BINARY}: Fastest (native CPU operations)
\item \textbf{DECIMAL}: Slower (BCD arithmetic instructions)
\item \textbf{DISPLAY}: Slowest (string parsing and conversion)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Loop Performance}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{FOR/NEXT}: Pre-allocated iteration count (fast)
\item \textbf{WHILE/WEND}: Condition evaluated each iteration (slightly slower)
\item \textbf{GOTO loops}: No optimization; same speed as WHILE
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Array Access Performance}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{1D arrays}: O(1) direct indexing
\item \textbf{2D arrays}: O(1) with row-major indexing
\item \textbf{3D+ arrays}: O(1) with flat storage calculation
\end{itemize}

\section{Debugging Hints}

\subsection{Common Errors}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Infinite loops}: Missing counter increment in WHILE, or bad exit condition
\item \textbf{Array out of bounds}: Forgetting array declaration or off-by-one errors
\item \textbf{Type overflow}: Assigning values > 9999 to DECIMAL types
\item \textbf{Undefined variable}: Using variable before LET assignment
\item \textbf{Missing RETURN}: Subroutine doesn't return, halts program
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Debugging Strategies}

\begin{itemize}
\item Add PRINT statements before/after key operations
\item Use REM for checkpoints: ``REM DEBUG: At line X, Z = ?''
\item Test loops with PRINT X to verify counter behavior
\item Verify array bounds with explicit range checks
\item Use GOSUB organization to isolate problem areas
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The 17 core BASIC statements provide sufficient capability to write educational, business logic, and game programming applications. By understanding numeric type handling, control flow patterns, and subroutine organization, programmers can create robust, portable BASIC programs that compile efficiently to EightBol intermediate representation and execute across multiple backend architectures (6502, Z80, ARM7, etc.).

Key takeaways:

\begin{itemize}
\item Choose numeric types carefully (BINARY for general use, DECIMAL for BCD, DISPLAY for strings)
\item Structure code with GOSUB/RETURN for readability and reuse
\item Use FOR/NEXT for iteration counts, WHILE/WEND for conditions
\item Initialize data with READ/DATA/RESTORE
\item Handle errors with IF/THEN/GOTO patterns
\item Test early and often with diagnostic PRINT statements
\end{itemize}

For further reference, consult the EightBol BASIC Frontend Language chapter for syntax details and the Backend-specific documentation for CPU-level behavior of compiled code.

